    "technique_notes": [
      {
        "heading": "Tuning: E\u266d Standard (Half Step Down)",
        "body": "Tune every string DOWN a half step: low E\u2192Eb, A\u2192Ab, D\u2192Db, G\u2192Gb, B\u2192Bb, high E\u2192Eb. SRV used this tuning across most of his catalog \u2014 you're still fingering E, A, and B shapes, but the guitar sounds a half step lower than written. Use a chromatic tuner and confirm each string; a guitar in standard will clash brutally with the record."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Texas Shuffle Double-Stops: The Core Engine",
        "body": "Every chord in this groove is driven by a pair of notes on adjacent strings \u2014 the 6th and 5th intervals that give Texas blues its greasy, two-voice feel. Keep your index finger planted on the lower note of each pair and add your ring finger for the 4th-fret hits; resist the urge to re-grip on every beat. The pattern is: open \u2192 2nd fret \u2192 4th fret \u2192 2nd fret, all as swung eighths \u2014 if it doesn't sound lopsided and hypnotic, you're not leaning far enough into the long-short swing ratio."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Thumb Bass Anchoring the Groove",
        "body": "SRV plucks the low open root (Eb, Ab, or Bb string depending on the chord) with his thumb on beat 1, then launches straight into the double-stop shuffle. That bass hit is an accent \u2014 dig in with the thumb so it snaps, don't treat it as an afterthought. If your thumb and fingers are fighting each other for timing, slow down and isolate the thumb-first attack until it locks before the double-stops enter."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Eb Blues Box: Fret-Hand Efficiency",
        "body": "The double-stop pairs live at frets 0, 2, and 4 on adjacent string pairs \u2014 this is your Eb blues box, and your index finger should own fret 2 as home. Never lift the index off the lower string when you jump to fret 4 with the ring finger; keep it hovering so you can land the return cleanly. At the IV chord (A7), the same shape migrates to the D and G strings \u2014 same geometry, new string pair, same two fingers."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Shuffle Feel: Swing the Eighths Hard",
        "body": "This tab is marked shuffle, so every pair of eighths is played long-short in a triplet ratio \u2014 the first eighth gets two thirds of the beat, the second gets one third. Do NOT subdivide literally as triplets when you play; just internalize 'bounce' and let the groove breathe. A common trap is evening out the eighths when you get tired or speed up \u2014 if the song stops feeling like it's rolling, your swing has collapsed into straight time."
      }
    ],
